use /lib
from /scope/editor import EditorScope
from /scope/prompt-mode/chat-input import ChatInputScope
from /ui/components/scroll-column import ScrollColumnScope

export scope MessageList:
    concept: {MessageListConcept}

concept MessageListConcept:
    pitch:
        The task view of prompt mode: the tasks sent to the harness and their
        output as the harness works on them.

    shape: {MessageListShape}

shape MessageListShape:
    description:
        Shows tasks and their output, not a conversation. Prompts aren't shown
        on the right with responses on the left, and tool calls aren't gathered
        into groups.

    sentPrompt:
        A task's header shows the compiled markdown that was actually sent to
        the harness, rendered as markdown, once it has compiled, along with the
        name of the hidden anchor it was compiled from.

    status:
        A task's status is a coloured label that spells it out: "Building" in
        blue, "Compiling" in cyan, "Running" in yellow, "Done" in green, "Failed" in red, and
        "Not recorded" in grey for a task from the history with no record of its
        run. The labels are readable in both light and dark mode.

    outputTable:
        A task's output is a table with Type, Output, and Status columns, with
        one row per thing the harness did, in order: each piece of reply text,
        each tool call, and any error.

        Type is a coloured badge with an icon. Reply text is "Reply" and errors
        are "Error". Tool calls are "Files" for Read, Glob, Grep, LS, and
        NotebookRead; "Edit" for Edit, MultiEdit, Write, and NotebookEdit;
        "Command" for Bash, BashOutput, KillShell, and KillBash; "Web" for
        WebFetch and WebSearch; "Agent" for Task and Agent; and "Tool" for
        anything else.

        A tool call's status is "running", "done", or "failed", and a call
        still running when the run ends takes the state the run ended in.

        Reply text is rendered as markdown, with no border around it. What
        subagents do is left out. If nothing streamed but the harness reports a
        final result, that result is shown as the reply. A finished task with
        nothing to show reads "No output."

    toolCalls:
        A tool call's Output cell shows its most telling argument. For Files,
        Edit, Command, Web, and Agent calls the tool's name is left out, since
        the badge already says what it is; any other tool's name comes first.

        Any path inside the project directory is shown relative to it, and the
        project directory itself reads `.`.

        A command is shown whole, as a syntax-highlighted bash block, and a
        long one is split across lines at `&&`, `||`, `|`, and `;`, one command
        per line with the chained ones indented. It never splits inside quotes,
        command substitutions, brackets, comments, or heredocs. Other arguments
        are shown on a single line.

    running:
        While a task is under way, its table ends in a row for whatever the
        harness does next. Until a row has something meaningful to show, its
        Type cell is a skeleton and its Output cell shows the last three lines
        of the harness's raw output: muted, monospace, JSON syntax-highlighted,
        and always three lines tall so the row doesn't jump. Before any output
        has arrived it reads "Waiting for the harness…". As soon as a row's
        type is known, its badge is real, and as soon as its text or argument
        arrives, that is shown. While the input of a Files, Edit, Command, Web,
        or Agent call is still arriving, its Output cell shows only a skeleton.

    runningSkeleton:
        A skeleton stands in for a currently running task only while there is
        nothing meaningful to show for it. As soon as there is anything
        meaningful to display for that task in the message list, no skeleton
        is shown for it at all.

    links:
        Links in the compiled prompt and in reply text are clickable. A link to
        a file opens it in the @{EditorScope} beside the task view, a web link
        opens in the browser, and a link to a file that doesn't exist shows an
        error notification. The file path on a Read, Edit, Write, or MultiEdit
        row is shown as a link and opens that file the same way.

    backgrounds:
        The header for the latest task is coloured like the tab the task was
        sent from, the same colour the @{ChatInputScope} body shows for that
        tab: a subtle red for Code, purple for Chain, and a subtle blue for
        Spec. A task from the history that doesn't say which tab it was sent
        from keeps the tab bar's colour. The queue along the bottom and the
        previous tasks row along the top both have the same background colour
        as the tab bar.

    askDimming:
        While a finished question from the Ask tab is open onto its full task
        table, a dark shade fades in over the message list to draw attention
        to it, and fades back out when it is closed or closed down to its row.
        The shade also shows while the previous answers are expanded on the Ask
        tab. Questions still running, each a single row, never dim the list.
        It covers only the message list, not a file open beside it, and doesn't
        block clicks.

    scrolling:
        The latest task's output scrolls with a @{ScrollColumnScope}, with its
        lock to the bottom. Unlocked, it still follows new output while it is
        already scrolled to the bottom. The lock is off until switched on.

    legibility:
        The output from a realtime agent should be readable and
        meaningful.  Make sure the font size is large enough, that the colours
        are legible.
